[THOUGHT] Smoking is a relevant risk factor — it impairs wound healing and immune response, potentially masking local signs of infection and increasing risk of postoperative complications (e.g., pneumonia, abscess formation). No travel, sick contacts, or occupational exposures makes community-acquired systemic infections less likely. We now move to **targeted physical examination** (domain 7), the first objective, discriminating layer assessment. In a post-cholecystectomy patient with rigors and fever, key exam elements include:  
- Vital signs (especially temperature, heart rate, BP, respiratory rate, oxygen saturation) — to assess for sepsis criteria;  
- Abdominal exam: tenderness (location — right upper quadrant vs. diffuse), rebound/guarding, bowel sounds, palpable mass, or fluid wave;  
- Surgical incision inspection (even if patient didn’t notice changes, clinician must verify);  
- Lung, skin, and cardiac exams to exclude alternative sources (e.g., atelectasis, cellulitis, endocarditis).  

Since I cannot perform the exam directly, I must request it as a structured clinical action.

[TEST] Perform and report vital signs (temperature, heart rate, blood pressure, respiratory rate, SpO₂), focused abdominal examination (tenderness location and severity, presence of rebound/guarding, bowel sounds, any mass or fluid wave), and detailed inspection of the surgical incision (erythema, induration, fluctuance, purulent drainage, dehiscence). Also assess lungs (for crackles or diminished breath sounds) and skin (for rashes or petechiae).

